\section*{Balanced L3 boundedness of the trilinear Hilbert transform}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
\textit{A trilinear Hilbert-transform estimate}: The manuscript claims an $L^1$ output bound for three $L^3(\mathbb R)$ inputs to the Hilbert transform with slopes $1,2,3$. This is a fixed-slope symmetric-exponent case.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
\textit{A trilinear Hilbert-transform estimate}: Local forms are compressed using projection and quadratic localization tools. A base form has few active scales on each path, and curvature localization splits it into side terms and a core. Square-energy bounds control the sides, counting bounds control the core, and the final parameter choices absorb the remaining errors.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
\textit{A trilinear Hilbert-transform estimate}: The primary statement says: The one-dimensional trilinear Hilbert transform with slopes one, two and three maps three L3 Schwartz inputs to L1. The target retains these quantified objects and hypotheses, so its positive assertion is covered.

With the hypotheses and conventions above, the claimed result addresses the whole mathematical assertion on this page.

\subsection{Formal verification}
\textit{A trilinear Hilbert-transform estimate}: The pinned catalogue lists no Lean formalization for this result.

This site has not run the Lean development. These links describe OpenAI’s selected formal statements and should be read with their stated scope.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI reports that an internal model produced the manuscripts. Their arguments have not been independently verified as proofs by this site. Any portion outside the documented formal scope may contain mistakes, and the catalogue label reports the announced mathematical claim.

